#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952} :activity/id #uuid "6fbac718-0db1-5d0a-8eac-581af3c3ed39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163262 :tutorial/content "We often want to know if there is a relationship between two random variables. For this reason, we wish to formulate the idea of a joint probability distribution.\n\nTo introduce this concept, suppose the random variable $X$ represents the number of heads obtained when a fair coin is flipped. The support of $X,$ denoted $S_X,$ consists of all possible values of $X$ and in this case is given by\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ represents the result of rolling a fair tetrahedral die. Then the support of $Y,$ denoted $S_Y,$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe **joint support** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $S,$ consists of all possible pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x$ is a possible outcome for $X$ and $y$ is a possible outcome for $Y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/34/34a8b2e96a6ef41d66c441aebe08d204a0bc84b210f8de04c836907f0afd9d29.svg)\n\nNote that $S_X\\times S_Y$ is the cartesian product of $S_X$ and $S_Y.$\n\nLet's assume that the flipping of the coin and rolling of the die are independent. Then, for any $x \\in X$ and any $y \\in Y,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\:\\textrm{and}\\:Y=y) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe **joint probability mass function** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $f(x,y),$ can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n\n \n\nSimilar to the case of single random variables, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).\n$$\n\nGoing forward, we will use the shorthand notation $P(X=x, Y=y)$ to mean $P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b99576-5a28-5c03-bc5d-1cc472ef0795" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5348 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9628dc39-75f0-5169-a945-48c68e6e4f31" :step/math-academy-id 33477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161863 :question/id #uuid "2d698f44-e551-58a2-883b-3a992119bd76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424258}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3e1d6fef-61c1-5a3a-805d-6a020df8bb08" :step/math-academy-id 34016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161873 :question/id #uuid "f2789f6c-11d5-5132-91b3-be65dac4ce24" :question/math-academy-id "e-9203"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e20c64-85be-5cb5-acde-17ffd8b6aff5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423190}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "b093ac87-86c2-5281-860f-df1321b270d4" :step/math-academy-id 34339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161874 :question/id #uuid "01b86f31-f31e-567d-b798-fc0f10a4d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424257}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "657c560d-1a7e-5600-8883-4eaa35b157cd" :step/math-academy-id 34343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163328 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. For a function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=P(X=x, Y=y)\n$$\n\n \n\nto be a valid joint probability mass function with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y) \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y)= 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y) \\in A \\right) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in A} f(x,y),$ where $A$ is a subset of $S.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y)\\in S$ states that any possible outcome for $(X,Y)$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by adding up the probabilities associated with $A.$\n\nThe joint probability mass function of a discrete random variable with finite support can be represented using a table, as shown below.\n\n|  | $Y=y_1$ | $Y=y_2$ | $\\ldots$ | $Y=y_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=x_1$ | $f(x_1,y_1)$ | $f(x_1,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_1,y_n)$ |\n| $X=x_2$ | $f(x_2,y_1)$ | $f(x_2,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_2,y_n)$ |\n| $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| $X=x_k$ | $f(x_k,y_1)$ | $f(x_k,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_k,y_n)$ |\n\nIn the case of two random variables, the joint distribution is sometimes called a **bivariate distribution**.\nHowever, the concept generalizes to any number of random variables, giving a **multivariate distribution**." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5858f9-f1f3-5d98-b436-9ffd034a3e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6284 :tutorial/title "Bivariate and Multivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "2636297d-b114-590c-9454-08ebab970ce2" :step/math-academy-id 38084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162162}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423193} :activity/id #uuid "364d0a99-928c-55de-b781-b3c99a34098f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172b0d28-9053-5c75-82a3-9176b2177f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423184} {:answer/id #uuid "19dfeb7e-c8d5-5045-9993-de85d2a62535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423185} {:answer/id #uuid "311d7f39-80c9-5bd3-b7a2-7108330abf41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423186} {:answer/id #uuid "80910aba-a6f7-5cea-a000-b98b3f7a7db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423187} {:answer/id #uuid "9c647d4a-3f89-51f3-80fe-0c630dad6a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423184} :answer-field/id #uuid "80684957-1816-5bbf-b499-0054dfab3f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d9a0e99d-ee2e-54a9-9f59-8e7f0b18bda7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110966" :question/problem "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.025$ | $0.01$ | $0.01$ | $0.025$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.01$ | $0.05$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ | $0.01$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.025$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate $P(Y-X=2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y-X=2) & =P(X=0,Y=2)+P(X=1,Y=3) \\\\  & +P(X=2,Y=4)+P(X=3,Y=5) \\\\  & =f(0,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,4)+f(3,5) \\\\  & =0.01+0.01+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.22.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c65c513-af22-54cc-8e3c-607c59a2146a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039b80b2-527f-56cc-a9bc-ff3bc90ac111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244338} {:answer/id #uuid "27f7a13e-be49-5079-b844-a4c3415b2fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244665} {:answer/id #uuid "adbb15ed-5e20-5e15-ac70-ff60a0a5d46d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245865} {:answer/id #uuid "b6826898-2df3-5edc-9c17-76d4e6512dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eb299c-829c-5764-a80a-06bf21a8c82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245945} :answer-field/id #uuid "89831f9f-6346-59a1-a3f3-bf51f497aaa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "89e9e61a-c7f2-587b-936c-fdec6e3defa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-109687" :question/problem "|  | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.12$ | $0.23$ |\n\nCalculate $P(X+Y=1)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y=1) & =P(X=0,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=0) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,0) \\\\  & =0.25+0.2 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6721940f-5f05-5fad-bcea-05d07a41f55c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd153f7-654f-5ed4-b9fe-3b1731a70ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241460} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa1370c-c82b-5fe3-ad6e-a777ec65e824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242039} {:answer/id #uuid "a54e62bf-fa8c-5810-a8eb-8f31624180ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} {:answer/id #uuid "c272c385-4c5e-5735-b854-1a68b031613e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242885} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8dd4bf-32b3-55ab-8c67-2219401fd8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242635} :answer-field/id #uuid "5497cc27-067a-5c7c-b637-6c6d86e6b1e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10ef8c30-25b6-5a94-b4c4-25e4aa370204" :question/math-academy-id "q-109336" :question/problem "|  | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2\\,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\,Y\\le 4) & =P((X,Y)\\in \\{(2,2),(2,4)\\}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(2,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,4)) \\\\  & =f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bb98f552-49b6-5d50-9a85-6fcbe8252a94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423192}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423183}]}
